WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.644 --> 00:00:16.308
When the Greek king of Thessaly, Peleus, married, he was so enamored of his beautiful wife, it never

2
00:00:16.368 --> 00:00:21.611
occurred to him that the gods were meddling in his fate—and that the gods would cause the death

3
00:00:21.651 --> 00:00:24.232
of his son, Achilles, on the plains of Troy.

4
00:00:25.113 --> 00:00:32.589
King Peleus' wife was Thetis, an immortal and beautiful sea nymph loved by Zeus, the most powerful of

5
00:00:32.629 --> 00:00:33.150
the gods.

6
00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:40.235
Thetis, however, had not returned Zeus' love, so he had sent her to earth to marry a mortal

7
00:00:40.356 --> 00:00:41.176
as punishment.

8
00:00:42.037 --> 00:00:46.481
Still, Thetis bore Peleus a son, whom they named Achilles.

9
00:00:47.301 --> 00:00:48.983
The Greek king was overjoyed.

10
00:00:49.703 --> 00:00:56.069
A seer revealed to Thetis that Achilles would be a great war hero, but rather than celebrate the

11
00:00:56.129 --> 00:00:59.752
news, Thetis worried that her son would die young in battle.

12
00:01:00.536 --> 00:01:06.721
Desperate to protect him, she carried Achilles away to the underworld, where the River Styx ran deep.

13
00:01:07.822 --> 00:01:15.449
The infernal river had mysterious powers, so Thetis held the boy above the rushing water, grasping him by

14
00:01:15.489 --> 00:01:18.311
his heel, and dipped him into it headfirst.

15
00:01:19.312 --> 00:01:26.658
Immersing Achilles in the magical waters would protect him from harm, but Thetis failed to notice that the

16
00:01:26.718 --> 00:01:28.480
heel she held did not get wet.

17
00:01:29.424 --> 00:01:34.705
This one spot untouched by the river became the only vulnerable place on his body.

18
00:01:35.466 --> 00:01:42.488
As Achilles was growing up, a war was brewing—its causes extending all the way back to Peleus' wedding.

19
00:01:43.268 --> 00:01:50.430
Thetis and Peleus had neglected to invite Eris, the goddess of discord, to their wedding banquet, and she

20
00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:52.590
arrived uninvited and incensed.

21
00:01:53.371 --> 00:01:58.492
She threw a golden apple among the guests with a message written on it: "For the fairest."

22
00:01:59.232 --> 00:02:07.535
Three goddesses—Hera, Athena, and Aphrodite—all reached for the apple at the same time, and an argument erupted among

23
00:02:07.576 --> 00:02:14.418
them. Zeus suggested that Paris, the young prince of Troy, should choose who was fairest to end the

24
00:02:14.458 --> 00:02:21.781
dispute. The three goddesses all tempted Paris with rewards—Hera with the chance to rule all of Europe and

25
00:02:21.821 --> 00:02:24.643
Asia, and Athena with the gift of wisdom.

26
00:02:25.703 --> 00:02:29.589
But the young prince selected Aphrodite, the goddess of love.

27
00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:34.793
Her reward was the love of Helen, the most beautiful woman in the world.

28
00:02:35.593 --> 00:02:41.677
Unfortunately, Helen was already married to the king of the Greek city of Sparta, Menelaus.

29
00:02:42.438 --> 00:02:49.423
True to her word, Aphrodite arranged for Paris to be invited to Menelaus' home, where he was introduced

30
00:02:49.483 --> 00:02:50.603
to the gorgeous queen.

31
00:02:51.424 --> 00:02:56.788
The moment the king left them alone, Paris abducted Helen and took her back to Troy.

32
00:02:57.652 --> 00:03:04.114
When Menelaus discovered what Paris had done, he called on his fellow Greek kings and, with his brother,

33
00:03:04.274 --> 00:03:09.475
Agamemnon, amassed a vast army that sailed for Troy in a thousand ships.

34
00:03:10.256 --> 00:03:16.697
The Trojan War was the greatest conflict the ancient world had seen, but a seer named Calchas had

35
00:03:16.757 --> 00:03:19.418
prophesied its outcome years before it began.

36
00:03:20.678 --> 00:03:25.880
Troy would be taken by the Greeks, but not without the help of the great warrior Achilles.

37
00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:33.244
When Thetis had first heard the seer's prediction, she was alarmed, and she sent her young son away

38
00:03:33.304 --> 00:03:36.926
to the court of King Lycomedes on the island of Skyros.

39
00:03:37.907 --> 00:03:45.651
There, Achilles disguised himself as a girl and lived peacefully for many years, but in secret, he taught

40
00:03:45.671 --> 00:03:49.733
himself to use a sword and shield and learned hand-to-hand combat.

41
00:03:50.794 --> 00:03:58.110
Agamemnon also knew of Calchas' prophecy, and he sent Odysseus, the cleverest of the Greek kings, to look

42
00:03:58.150 --> 00:03:58.850
for Achilles.

43
00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:06.133
When the wily Odysseus arrived at Skyros with his men, he arranged a test to draw Achilles out

44
00:04:06.153 --> 00:04:07.173
from among the women.

45
00:04:08.194 --> 00:04:14.236
Odysseus ordered two carts to be set in the city plaza, one filled with fine clothes and perfume,

46
00:04:14.876 --> 00:04:16.357
the other with weapons of war.

47
00:04:17.297 --> 00:04:23.948
Achilles' interest in fighting betrayed him; all the women lunged for the clothes in the first cart, but

48
00:04:23.988 --> 00:04:25.529
Achilles went to the second.

49
00:04:26.389 --> 00:04:33.332
Odysseus recognized Achilles and convinced him to join the Greek expedition and lead an army against Troy.

50
00:04:34.032 --> 00:04:38.574
When the Greeks arrived at Troy, they discovered the city was heavily fortified.

51
00:04:39.434 --> 00:04:45.677
The armies battled on the plains before the city walls, but the Greeks could not penetrate Troy's defenses.

52
00:04:46.497 --> 00:04:51.259
Frustrated, the Greeks attacked and destroyed 23 towns surrounding Troy.

53
00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:58.519
In one of these, Lyrnessos, Achilles fell in love with a woman named Briseis and took her captive.

54
00:04:59.459 --> 00:05:02.161
But Agamemnon claimed Briseis for himself.

55
00:05:03.001 --> 00:05:09.705
Irate at losing Briseis, Achilles refused to fight, staying in his tent by the Greek ships and keeping

56
00:05:09.745 --> 00:05:11.506
his soldiers from the battle as well.

57
00:05:12.407 --> 00:05:18.150
In the following days, the war went poorly for the Greeks, and the Trojans battled them back from

58
00:05:18.190 --> 00:05:18.930
the city walls.

59
00:05:19.971 --> 00:05:27.006
Hoping to turn the tide in the Greeks' favor, Achilles' close friend Patroclus dressed himself in Achilles' armor

60
00:05:27.547 --> 00:05:31.329
and, carrying Achilles' sword and shield, led his soldiers into battle.

61
00:05:32.329 --> 00:05:37.992
Believing the mighty Achilles had returned, the Trojan soldiers fell back—except for one.

62
00:05:38.012 --> 00:05:42.215
Hector was Troy's great hero, and the brother of Paris.

63
00:05:42.975 --> 00:05:48.618
He boldly challenged Patroclus, thinking the young man in front of him was Achilles, and killed him.

64
00:05:49.498 --> 00:05:54.381
He stripped the corpse of Achilles' armor and weapons and triumphantly returned to Troy.

65
00:05:55.282 --> 00:05:58.164
Achilles vowed to take revenge for the death of his friend.

66
00:05:58.945 --> 00:06:03.088
He asked Hephaestus to craft a new set of armor and weapons for him.

67
00:06:03.968 --> 00:06:08.071
Then, Achilles sent a message to Hector that he had killed the wrong man.

68
00:06:08.091 --> 00:06:15.517
Hector knew that his chances of winning against the powerful Achilles and his enchanted armor were slim, but

69
00:06:15.557 --> 00:06:19.860
he dutifully dressed for battle once again and strode out of the city gates.

70
00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:27.085
The bloody clash between the two heroes left every soldier, Greek and Trojan alike, staring in awe.

71
00:06:27.886 --> 00:06:32.090
At last, Achilles killed Hector and avenged his slain friend.

72
00:06:32.870 --> 00:06:35.232
Achilles' grief, however, was undiminished.

73
00:06:35.893 --> 00:06:42.238
He fastened straps to Hector's body, attached them to his chariot, and dragged the fallen hero around the

74
00:06:42.298 --> 00:06:42.919
city walls.

75
00:06:43.859 --> 00:06:49.224
Priam, King of Troy and Hector's father, begged Achilles to stop, but to no avail.

76
00:06:50.204 --> 00:06:57.369
Finally, after Achilles had circled the city three times, he relented, and Priam took his son's remains away.

77
00:06:58.290 --> 00:07:03.493
Achilles then returned to the battlefield and fought heroically through the long years of the war.

78
00:07:04.454 --> 00:07:08.817
However, the gods Poseidon and Apollo had been watching the war closely.

79
00:07:09.598 --> 00:07:16.262
They supported opposite sides, but they were both infuriated by Achilles' arrogant desecration of Hector's body.

80
00:07:17.223 --> 00:07:18.564
They decided to intervene.

81
00:07:19.204 --> 00:07:24.266
Even though the Greeks went on to win the war, Achilles did not survive to see victory.

82
00:07:25.227 --> 00:07:28.448
Poseidon informed Paris of Achilles' unprotected heel.

83
00:07:29.188 --> 00:07:33.550
From the high walls of Troy, the prince searched for Achilles in the battle below.

84
00:07:33.570 --> 00:07:39.713
He fired an arrow at Achilles' heel, and Apollo guided the arrow to the vulnerable spot.

85
00:07:40.673 --> 00:07:46.676
Achilles fell to the ground, writhing in pain, and moments later, the great warrior was dead.

86
00:07:47.780 --> 00:07:53.185
Achilles' corpse was burned on a huge pyre and his ashes buried near his parents' home.

87
00:07:54.105 --> 00:08:00.210
Before his body was consumed in the flames, Thetis snatched her son's soul from the pyre and brought

88
00:08:00.250 --> 00:08:01.511
it to the island of Leuce.

89
00:08:02.612 --> 00:08:06.595
There, he rejoined his dead friends, including his beloved Patroclus.
